From ab20455635bf8aa8ae5ef1584774129db1034261 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:54:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 194/206] [vm] F162: resolve commit-message/code mismatch in
 the native-call escape guard
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

d10ab819892's commit message claimed the kRunDartOnCPUCode native-call
escape guard in DecodeSimulatorToNativeTransition checks against
sim->overflow_stack_limit(), "the same" floor as dart_entry.cc's
InvokeDartCode reentry guard. The code it actually shipped uses
OSThread::Current()->overflow_stack_limit() instead -- a real mismatch,
caught by an independent falsification pass (see memo.md 2026-10-01
"F162 修复独立证伪 pass 回收: PARTIAL"), which also found that pass's
repro (f162_repro/n3.dart) never actually drives execution through this
site, only through dart_entry.cc's.

This resolves it with new evidence rather than picking a side:

- Added ShorebirdSimToCpu_DeepRecursion_SiteReachable and
  ShorebirdSimToCpu_DeepRecursion_ToFloor to shorebird_sim_to_cpu_test.cc:
  a reusable brk-#kRunDartOnCPUCode code buffer whose native target
  reenters the Simulator (sim->Call) on itself with depth-1, driving a
  real nested Sim(brk)->CPU(native)->Sim(brk)->... chain that consumes
  genuine OS C stack per level -- the same shape the production escape
  hits under deep recursive Dart code calling back into itself through
  an indirect static call table entry that resolves to CPU code (see
  shorebird/wrapper.cc SimToCPUConfig), which f162_repro/n3.dart's
  noSuchMethod chain cannot reach because that only drives
  dart_entry.cc's reentry.
- Site reachability was confirmed with a one-off hit-counter
  instrumented directly at the guard (since reverted, not part of this
  commit): depth=3 produced exactly 4 hits (one per sim->Call level,
  including the depth==0 base case), ruling out the decoder dispatching
  somewhere else.
- At depth=2000 the shipped code (OSThread floor) completes cleanly; at
  depth=20000 it FATALs with a controlled diagnostic (exit 134/SIGABRT).
- Negative control: temporarily disabling the guard (`if (false && ...)`)
  at depth=20000 produces a real, uncontrolled SIGSEGV (exit 139) with no
  diagnostic -- confirming the guard is load-bearing on this site, not
  just decorative.
- Trying the commit message's claimed floor (sim->overflow_stack_limit())
  in place of OSThread's reproduces a false positive: it FATALs even at
  depth=2000, which the shipped floor passes cleanly, because at this
  exact brk trap execution is always still inside Simulator::Execute's
  interpreter loop (confirmed by reading GenerateTransitionDartToCpuIfNeeded
  Stub: its cbz only brk's while Thread::is_simulating_ is true, and
  tail-jumps directly otherwise) -- i.e. on the real OS C stack, not on
  Simulator's own heap-allocated stack buffer, so sim->overflow_stack_limit()
  names a disjoint, irrelevant address range here.

Conclusion: the shipped code was correct; the commit message was wrong.
Documents this in a code comment at the guard site (does not amend
d10ab819892, per this project's new-commits-not-amends policy) and adds
the two tests above as permanent regression coverage for this site.

Verified: runtime_check p1-p8 parity suite still 8/8 against
ProductSimARM64_cpu's own gen_snapshot/dartaotruntime, in both default
(FLAG_use_simulator=true) and --no-use_simulator modes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/shorebird_sim_to_cpu_test.cc b/runtime/vm/shorebird_sim_to_cpu_test.cc
index b8d26f61ad7..91f3178bebd 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/shorebird_sim_to_cpu_test.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/shorebird_sim_to_cpu_test.cc
@@ -167,5 +167,135 @@ ISOLATE_UNIT_TEST_CASE(ShorebirdSimToCpu_HypercallBlockIsContiguous) {
   EXPECT(Simulator::NameOfSimToCpuHypercall(Instr::kBreakPointCode) == nullptr);
 }
 
+// F162: the kRunDartOnCPUCode native-call escape (see constants_arm64.h,
+// simulator_arm64.cc:DecodeSimulatorToNativeTransition) is independent of
+// InvokeDartCode's CPU-shape reentry (dart_entry.cc) but shares the same
+// hazard: it sets simulator_csp_ to the *real* OS C stack pointer right
+// before the native call it makes (InvokeWithTHR), and if that native call
+// itself re-enters the simulator (sim->Call again), real C stack is consumed
+// on every level with no bound. These two tests build a reusable code
+// buffer identical in shape to ShorebirdSimToCpu_RunDartOnCpuHypercall above
+// and drive deep *reentrant* recursion through exactly this escape -- not
+// through dart_entry.cc's path, which the existing f162_repro/n3.dart only
+// reaches -- to confirm this site is reachable under real recursion and that
+// its guard is load-bearing there. (The question of which floor,
+// sim->overflow_stack_limit() vs OSThread::Current()->overflow_stack_limit(),
+// is correct for this site was resolved by a one-off falsification-pass
+// instrumentation that is not part of this permanent test: swapping to
+// sim->overflow_stack_limit() made the guard FATAL at depth 2000, which the
+// OSThread floor passes cleanly, because at this exact brk trap the escape
+// is always reached while genuinely interpreting -- see the cbz in
+// GenerateTransitionDartToCpuIfNeededStub, which only brk's when still
+// interpreting and tail-jumps directly otherwise -- so the real OS C stack,
+// not the Simulator's own heap buffer, is what is being consumed.)
+
+namespace {
+
+// Builds one page of code: brk #kRunDartOnCPUCode with x16 pointed at
+// `native_target`, x0 left alone (the caller sets it via sim->Call's first
+// parameter register). Identical shape to the code buffer in
+// ShorebirdSimToCpu_RunDartOnCpuHypercall, factored out so both new tests can
+// reuse the same allocation across thousands of reentrant calls instead of
+// mapping a fresh page per recursion level.
+std::unique_ptr<VirtualMemory> BuildRunDartOnCpuEscapeCode(uword native_target) {
+  std::unique_ptr<VirtualMemory> mem(VirtualMemory::Allocate(
+      VirtualMemory::PageSize(), false, false, "f162_deep_recursion"));
+  ASSERT(mem != nullptr);
+  uint32_t* code = reinterpret_cast<uint32_t*>(mem->address());
+  const uint64_t addr = static_cast<uint64_t>(native_target);
+  code[0] = 0xD2800010u | ((addr & 0xFFFF) << 5);            // movz x16, lo16
+  code[1] = 0xF2A00010u | (((addr >> 16) & 0xFFFF) << 5);    // movk x16, [31:16]
+  code[2] = 0xF2C00010u | (((addr >> 32) & 0xFFFF) << 5);    // movk x16, [47:32]
+  code[3] = 0xF2E00010u | (((addr >> 48) & 0xFFFF) << 5);    // movk x16, [63:48]
+  code[4] = BRK | (Instr::kRunDartOnCPUCode << kImm16Shift);
+  // Unreachable (tail transfer continues from LR, as in the test above);
+  // poison x0 so a fallthrough is visible rather than silently "working".
+  code[5] = 0xD2800000u | (77u << 5);                        // movz x0, #77
+  code[6] = 0xD65F03C0u;                                      // ret
+  mem->Protect(VirtualMemory::kReadExecute);
+  return mem;
+}
+
+VirtualMemory* g_f162_deep_recursion_code = nullptr;
+
+// Native target for the hypercall: if depth > 0, re-enters the Simulator via
+// sim->Call on the *same* code buffer with depth-1, i.e. a real reentrant
+// Sim(brk)->CPU(this function)->Sim(brk)->... chain, exactly the shape the
+// production escape hits under deep recursive Dart code calling back into
+// itself through an indirect static call table entry that resolves to CPU
+// code (see runtime/vm/shorebird/wrapper.cc SimToCPUConfig). If depth <= 0,
+// bottoms out and returns 0 so the whole chain unwinds.
+int64_t ShorebirdTestDeepRecursiveNativeCaller(int64_t depth,
+    int64_t, int64_t, int64_t, int64_t, int64_t, int64_t, int64_t) {
+  if (depth <= 0) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+  Simulator* const sim = Simulator::Current();
+  ASSERT(sim != nullptr);
+  return sim->Call(
+      static_cast<int64_t>(
+          reinterpret_cast<uword>(g_f162_deep_recursion_code->address())),
+      depth - 1, 0, 0, 0);
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
+// Sanity check (shallow, depth=3): the reentrant code buffer above must
+// actually drive execution through DecodeSimulatorToNativeTransition's
+// kRunDartOnCPUCode arm `depth + 1` times (once per level, including the
+// depth==0 base-case call that still brk's before the native target decides
+// not to recurse further) before any deep-recursion result from it is
+// trustworthy. This was verified with a one-off counter instrumented
+// directly at the guard site (since reverted, per F162's falsification-pass
+// instructions); what is pinned here is the observable behavior that
+// instrumentation confirmed: the chain completes with the right result at a
+// shallow depth with no crash and no spurious FATAL.
+ISOLATE_UNIT_TEST_CASE(ShorebirdSimToCpu_DeepRecursion_SiteReachable) {
+  Simulator* sim = Simulator::Current();
+  const uword target =
+      reinterpret_cast<uword>(&ShorebirdTestDeepRecursiveNativeCaller);
+  std::unique_ptr<VirtualMemory> mem = BuildRunDartOnCpuEscapeCode(target);
+  g_f162_deep_recursion_code = mem.get();
+
+  const int64_t result = sim->Call(
+      static_cast<int64_t>(reinterpret_cast<uword>(mem->address())), 3, 0, 0,
+      0);
+  EXPECT_EQ(0, result);
+
+  g_f162_deep_recursion_code = nullptr;
+}
+
+// Deep-recursion trial: drives the SAME reentrant chain as the sanity check
+// above to a depth read from F162_DEPTH (env var, default 20000), high
+// enough to either (a) hit the guard's FATAL() before any real crash, or
+// (b) actually SIGSEGV/SIGBUS if the guard's floor is wrong for this site.
+// This test is meant to be invoked as its own process
+// (`run_vm_tests ShorebirdSimToCpu_DeepRecursion_ToFloor`) and its outcome
+// read from the process exit status, not from in-process EXPECT -- a crash
+// takes the whole process down, which is the point: we want to see whether
+// that crash is a controlled FATAL (SIGABRT, Dart's FATAL calls abort()) or
+// an uncontrolled SIGSEGV/SIGBUS at an unrelated address.
+ISOLATE_UNIT_TEST_CASE(ShorebirdSimToCpu_DeepRecursion_ToFloor) {
+  Simulator* sim = Simulator::Current();
+  const uword target =
+      reinterpret_cast<uword>(&ShorebirdTestDeepRecursiveNativeCaller);
+  std::unique_ptr<VirtualMemory> mem = BuildRunDartOnCpuEscapeCode(target);
+  g_f162_deep_recursion_code = mem.get();
+
+  intptr_t depth = 20000;
+  if (const char* env = getenv("F162_DEPTH")) {
+    depth = atoll(env);
+  }
+  OS::PrintErr("F162_DEEP_RECURSION_START depth=%" Pd "\n", depth);
+  const int64_t result = sim->Call(
+      static_cast<int64_t>(reinterpret_cast<uword>(mem->address())),
+      static_cast<int64_t>(depth), 0, 0, 0);
+  // If we get here, the whole chain unwound without crashing or FATALing.
+  OS::PrintErr("F162_DEEP_RECURSION_COMPLETED result=%" Pd64 "\n", result);
+  EXPECT_EQ(0, result);
+
+  g_f162_deep_recursion_code = nullptr;
+}
+
 }  // namespace dart
 #endif  // TARGET_ARCH_ARM64 && DART_INCLUDE_SIMULATOR
diff --git a/runtime/vm/simulator_arm64.cc b/runtime/vm/simulator_arm64.cc
index a7454887a33..d92cd648e6d 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/simulator_arm64.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/simulator_arm64.cc
@@ -2171,6 +2171,23 @@ bool Simulator::DecodeSimulatorToNativeTransition(Instr* instr) {
         // keeps tripping this native-call trap), it marches the real CSP
         // down with zero margin checked, and eventually collides with the
         // thread's stack guard page. Refuse rather than silently corrupt.
+        // F162 follow-up (independent falsification pass, see
+        // shorebird_sim_to_cpu_test.cc ShorebirdSimToCpu_DeepRecursion_*):
+        // this commit's own message says "against sim->overflow_stack_limit()",
+        // but the floor below is deliberately OSThread's, not sim's, and that
+        // is correct for this site -- it is NOT the same hazard shape as
+        // dart_entry.cc's reentry. GenerateTransitionDartToCpuIfNeededStub
+        // only reaches this brk while Thread::is_simulating_ is still true
+        // (its cbz tail-jumps directly, without a brk, once already on the
+        // CPU), so DecodeSimulatorToNativeTransition's kRunDartOnCPUCode arm
+        // is always entered from inside Simulator::Execute's C++ interpreter
+        // loop -- i.e. on the real OS C stack, not on Simulator's own
+        // heap-allocated stack buffer. Swapping this floor to
+        // sim->overflow_stack_limit() was tried empirically and FATALs
+        // spuriously at a depth (2000) the OSThread floor passes cleanly,
+        // because sim->overflow_stack_limit() names an address range
+        // entirely disjoint from current_csp here. The commit message is the
+        // thing that was wrong, not the code.
         OSThread* os_thread = OSThread::Current();
         const uword current_csp = OSThread::GetCurrentStackPointer();
         const uword floor =
-- 
2.53.0

